.he "sh (1)                       8/12/26                       sh (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
sh
- the micronix shell
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
sh [ -v ] [ -c string ] [ file ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Sh reads lines and runs them.  With a file it reads that; with -c it
runs the one string it is given and stops; with neither it reads the
standard input, and prompts if that is a terminal.
.sp
A line is a pipeline: commands separated by a pipe, with redirections
and perhaps an ampersand on the end.  Each command is looked up as a
builtin first and then along the path, which is the current directory,
/bin and /usr/bin.
.sp
This is a csh rather than a Bourne shell.  The operators ending in an
ampersand send the standard error wherever the standard output is
going, and there is no here document and no way to redirect a numbered
descriptor.
.sp
A shell whose name begins with a minus is a login shell.  It reads
/.login and then /.sh before it reads anything else.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-v	Write each line to the standard error as it is run.

.ti -10
-c	Run the string that follows, and exit.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPERATORS
.in +5
.br
Longest first, which is the order they are matched in:
.sp
.nf
     |&    pipe the standard output and the error
     |     pipe the standard output
     &     run the pipeline in the background
     >>&   append both to a file
     >>    append the standard output to a file
     >&    both to a file
     >     the standard output to a file
     <     the standard input from a file
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUILTINS
.in +5
.br
.nf
     cd, chdir  change directory
     home       what cd with no argument means
     dir        list a directory
     era        remove files
     echo       write the arguments
     type       where a command would be run from
     path       set or show the search path
     prompt     set the prompt
     alias      make or show an abbreviation
     unalias    take one away
     source     read a file as input
     nice       run the rest more politely
     kill       signal a process
     wait       wait for the background ones
     pid        the process id
     sync       flush the buffer cache
     exit       exit, with a status if given
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
.nf
     /.login    read by a login shell, first
     /.sh       read after /.login
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
There is no source for the shell this tree ships in /bin, so this one
was read back out of that binary: the operator table, the builtin
numbering, the search path and the prompt are all its own.  Where the
two can be told apart, cmd/sh/NOTES says so.
.sp
It installs as /bin/nsh rather than over /bin/sh, so a system that
will not come up under it still comes up.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
msh (1)
